WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.350
[Instructor] - All right, so next up,

00:01.350 --> 00:03.750
we're gonna learn how to make our first branch.

00:03.750 --> 00:05.910
And we actually use the same command

00:05.910 --> 00:08.130
that we just saw, git branch,

00:08.130 --> 00:10.800
except now we add on a branch name.

00:10.800 --> 00:12.960
So git branch with a branch name

00:12.960 --> 00:15.000
will create a branch with that name

00:15.000 --> 00:17.370
versus git branch without anything else

00:17.370 --> 00:20.100
just lists the current branches.

00:20.100 --> 00:21.780
So git branch, branch name.

00:21.780 --> 00:23.280
And your branch name,

00:23.280 --> 00:24.870
we'll talk a little bit more about later,

00:24.870 --> 00:26.670
but it shouldn't include spaces.

00:26.670 --> 00:28.680
Let's just start with that.

00:28.680 --> 00:31.680
And generally, you want it to make sense and be informative,

00:31.680 --> 00:33.210
but you don't need to write, you know,

00:33.210 --> 00:35.670
a whole sentence to explain what the branch is.

00:35.670 --> 00:39.060
Also, this simply makes a branch for us.

00:39.060 --> 00:41.640
It does not switch to the branch.

00:41.640 --> 00:43.830
So I'm on my master branch here,

00:43.830 --> 00:46.290
as you can see, head points at master.

00:46.290 --> 00:48.570
I have two commits on the master branch.

00:48.570 --> 00:51.840
When I run this command, git branch bugfix,

00:51.840 --> 00:54.990
that makes me a new branch called bugfix.

00:54.990 --> 00:58.230
It's based off of the head where I currently was.

00:58.230 --> 01:00.723
So it points to the same exact spot.

01:01.590 --> 01:02.940
But I have a new branch.

01:02.940 --> 01:04.770
I have not switched to it.

01:04.770 --> 01:06.600
I'm still on master.

01:06.600 --> 01:08.850
So I'll demonstrate this to you.

01:08.850 --> 01:10.680
Let's make a new branch.

01:10.680 --> 01:12.360
But first, I'm actually gonna work on

01:12.360 --> 01:14.760
a separate project, a separate repo.

01:14.760 --> 01:17.017
Instead of working on the novel we were writing,

01:17.017 --> 01:20.040
"The Great Catsby," I'm gonna make a new repo.

01:20.040 --> 01:22.110
I'm in a folder, I'm just calling it branching,

01:22.110 --> 01:23.970
and I'm gonna make a directory.

01:23.970 --> 01:27.123
I'm gonna make a road trip playlist folder.

01:28.440 --> 01:31.710
So often, when I'm teaching this stuff, I'm torn

01:31.710 --> 01:34.740
if I should do something very easy to follow along with,

01:34.740 --> 01:36.360
something simple to understand,

01:36.360 --> 01:38.640
or if I should do something realistic.

01:38.640 --> 01:39.780
I'm gonna try and do both,

01:39.780 --> 01:42.270
but I'm gonna start with a super simple,

01:42.270 --> 01:43.950
easy-to-understand project.

01:43.950 --> 01:45.270
So it's not really a project.

01:45.270 --> 01:48.180
We're going on a road trip and I want a playlist of songs,

01:48.180 --> 01:50.100
and I'm gonna use git for whatever reason

01:50.100 --> 01:53.730
to track those songs and I'll make some different branches.

01:53.730 --> 01:56.250
So I'm gonna start by making my repository.

01:56.250 --> 01:58.320
So I'm in a folder, road trip playlist.

01:58.320 --> 02:00.240
I'm gonna run git in it.

02:00.240 --> 02:01.800
So I have an empty repo.

02:01.800 --> 02:03.780
I know that I wasn't in a git repo,

02:03.780 --> 02:06.720
but I should have checked to see with git status,

02:06.720 --> 02:08.250
but I know that I wasn't.

02:08.250 --> 02:11.040
And then I'm going to go ahead and make a file.

02:11.040 --> 02:15.510
I'm gonna call it playlist.txt.

02:15.510 --> 02:17.880
And I'll open this up in my editor.

02:17.880 --> 02:19.710
Okay, so here it is.

02:19.710 --> 02:22.530
I'm just gonna start by adding a very simple heading,

02:22.530 --> 02:25.590
like song-artist.

02:25.590 --> 02:29.550
So it's just the header heading for the playlist, right?

02:29.550 --> 02:33.030
I'm gonna add that file, git add playlist, git commit.

02:33.030 --> 02:35.878
I'm on the master branch, of course, just by default.

02:35.878 --> 02:39.450
Add playlist header.

02:39.450 --> 02:41.070
All right, we type git log,

02:41.070 --> 02:44.250
we see head is pointing to the master branch.

02:44.250 --> 02:46.020
Now, I'm gonna add two songs,

02:46.020 --> 02:49.320
just two ABBA songs to start that I happen to like.

02:49.320 --> 02:50.153
There they are.

02:50.153 --> 02:54.570
"SOS," mm, great song, "One of Us," also great song.

02:54.570 --> 02:55.740
I'm going to add those.

02:55.740 --> 02:58.710
I just wanna have one or two commits on my master branch.

02:58.710 --> 03:03.180
So I'm gonna do git add playlist, git commit,

03:03.180 --> 03:05.940
add two ABBA songs.

03:05.940 --> 03:06.773
All righty?

03:06.773 --> 03:09.120
So I'm on the master branch, right?

03:09.120 --> 03:10.410
Head points to master.

03:10.410 --> 03:11.760
We have two commits.

03:11.760 --> 03:13.800
Now let's make a new branch.

03:13.800 --> 03:15.300
For whatever reason I decide,

03:15.300 --> 03:17.490
I wanna make a different playlist

03:17.490 --> 03:20.760
based on this one that only contains oldies.

03:20.760 --> 03:22.620
So maybe I'm not sure, you know,

03:22.620 --> 03:24.360
what I want on my main playlist.

03:24.360 --> 03:26.250
So I'm gonna try a couple of options,

03:26.250 --> 03:28.170
and that's exactly what I'm going to do

03:28.170 --> 03:29.490
is make some different branches,

03:29.490 --> 03:31.620
and I'm gonna make a branch called oldies.

03:31.620 --> 03:34.080
And it's just gonna include songs that are,

03:34.080 --> 03:35.910
they're not technically what the radio

03:35.910 --> 03:37.020
would classify as oldies,

03:37.020 --> 03:41.250
but let's just say anything from before, like, 2000,

03:41.250 --> 03:43.710
which I recognize for most people is not oldies.

03:43.710 --> 03:48.360
So to make a branch, git branch and then a branch name.

03:48.360 --> 03:51.210
So let's make that oldies branch, git branch,

03:51.210 --> 03:53.130
and I'll make this full screen.

03:53.130 --> 03:54.660
And then actually before I do that,

03:54.660 --> 03:56.520
let's just do git branch,

03:56.520 --> 03:58.440
we're on master, it's the only branch.

03:58.440 --> 04:02.823
Now if I do git branch oldies and hit Enter,

04:04.110 --> 04:05.580
hmm, doesn't seem like anything happened.

04:05.580 --> 04:07.410
I'm still on master, right?

04:07.410 --> 04:09.570
If I type git status, I'm still on master.

04:09.570 --> 04:10.890
No surprise there.

04:10.890 --> 04:12.570
It doesn't switch us over.

04:12.570 --> 04:14.820
But if I type git branch,

04:14.820 --> 04:16.680
we do see we have a new branch,

04:16.680 --> 04:19.050
oldies, we're just not on it.

04:19.050 --> 04:22.162
Head is still pointing to master.

04:22.162 --> 04:24.150
You can see that again, type git log,

04:24.150 --> 04:25.920
head is pointing to master,

04:25.920 --> 04:28.560
but now we have this new reference, oldies,

04:28.560 --> 04:31.020
it's at the same commit, right?

04:31.020 --> 04:34.590
Two branch references pointing to the same thing.

04:34.590 --> 04:36.810
We just happen to be on master.

04:36.810 --> 04:39.840
So we're at this stage, two branches,

04:39.840 --> 04:41.490
they're both referring to the same point,

04:41.490 --> 04:44.370
the same commit, and we're on master.

04:44.370 --> 04:45.660
So to switch branches,

04:45.660 --> 04:49.200
there's a command that we can use called git switch.

04:49.200 --> 04:51.630
So this is actually a newer command in Git.

04:51.630 --> 04:53.310
I'll do a separate video.

04:53.310 --> 04:56.730
On the older command, we used something called git checkout

04:56.730 --> 04:57.990
to do the same thing.

04:57.990 --> 04:59.370
We'll talk about that.

04:59.370 --> 05:01.170
It's still used in other contexts,

05:01.170 --> 05:04.860
but git switch was introduced to switch between branches.

05:04.860 --> 05:07.140
So git switch and then a branch name.

05:07.140 --> 05:08.850
So in our example here,

05:08.850 --> 05:11.160
if I do git switch bugfix,

05:11.160 --> 05:14.850
head is now pointing to that bugfix branch.

05:14.850 --> 05:17.640
It was on master, now it's on bugfix.

05:17.640 --> 05:20.130
So in our case, we have this oldies branch,

05:20.130 --> 05:21.120
we are not on it.

05:21.120 --> 05:22.680
We see that we're on master.

05:22.680 --> 05:25.050
I type git status, we're on master.

05:25.050 --> 05:27.690
But if I do, git switch oldies,

05:27.690 --> 05:29.220
and I do need to spell it correctly.

05:29.220 --> 05:31.320
So if I reference something that doesn't exist,

05:31.320 --> 05:33.570
it tells me, there's no branch with that name.

05:33.570 --> 05:36.093
But if I do git switch oldies,

05:37.260 --> 05:39.810
hey, switch to branch oldies,

05:39.810 --> 05:42.210
my terminal shows oldies over here.

05:42.210 --> 05:47.130
If I type git status on branch oldies and if I type git log,

05:47.130 --> 05:50.820
you can now see head is pointing to oldies.

05:50.820 --> 05:53.640
Still the same commit though, I haven't done any new work.

05:53.640 --> 05:56.970
However, if I do some new work, I make a new commit here,

05:56.970 --> 05:58.530
this is the oldies playlist.

05:58.530 --> 06:00.030
So what should we add?

06:00.030 --> 06:04.597
Let's add two good, great George Jones songs.

06:04.597 --> 06:05.760
"He Stopped Loving Her Today."

06:05.760 --> 06:06.593
Ugh, great song.

06:06.593 --> 06:07.920
Great song.

06:07.920 --> 06:10.020
Okay, so I'm gonna save,

06:10.020 --> 06:11.580
and I'm going to commit.

06:11.580 --> 06:13.830
Now, when I commit here,

06:13.830 --> 06:15.750
remember, I'm on the oldies branch.

06:15.750 --> 06:20.250
So git add playlist, git commit, dash m,

06:20.250 --> 06:25.250
add two George Jones songs, just like that.

06:27.900 --> 06:30.540
And we just made our first commit on a new branch.

06:30.540 --> 06:32.370
We're on the oldies branch,

06:32.370 --> 06:33.870
and if I type git log,

06:33.870 --> 06:36.060
we're going to see something we haven't yet seen.

06:36.060 --> 06:38.400
We see head referring to oldies.

06:38.400 --> 06:40.080
That's what we expect to see.

06:40.080 --> 06:41.370
It's our most recent commit.

06:41.370 --> 06:44.520
That's where oldies is 'cause we're on the oldies branch.

06:44.520 --> 06:47.190
But we see master now has been left behind

06:47.190 --> 06:48.390
on this previous commit.

06:48.390 --> 06:50.310
Master is still, remember,

06:50.310 --> 06:52.350
this idea of a bookmark in the book.

06:52.350 --> 06:54.180
It's still bookmarking this commit.

06:54.180 --> 06:56.640
This is master, this is oldies,

06:56.640 --> 06:58.890
and we happen to be on the oldies branch.

06:58.890 --> 07:02.160
But I can switch, as we've seen, with git switch.

07:02.160 --> 07:04.320
So if I switch right now,

07:04.320 --> 07:06.840
this is what we have on our oldies branch.

07:06.840 --> 07:11.840
If I switch over, git switch to master, take a look.

07:12.480 --> 07:16.110
Now we have just two lines, just two songs rather.

07:16.110 --> 07:19.170
We don't have the George Jones stuff that we did.

07:19.170 --> 07:21.870
If I type git log on the master branch,

07:21.870 --> 07:23.790
we only have these two commits.

07:23.790 --> 07:26.580
Add the playlist header, add two ABBA songs.

07:26.580 --> 07:30.183
But when I switch back, git switch to oldies,

07:31.020 --> 07:34.470
we now have our two George Jones songs in addition,

07:34.470 --> 07:37.410
and I type git log, we have that extra commit.

07:37.410 --> 07:38.790
So that was just our first example

07:38.790 --> 07:40.380
of making a branch and switching.

07:40.380 --> 07:42.930
We will get more practice in the next video,

07:42.930 --> 07:44.160
but just to remind you,

07:44.160 --> 07:48.480
git branch on its own will list branches,

07:48.480 --> 07:51.000
git branch with a branch name makes a branch,

07:51.000 --> 07:52.740
and then to switch to a branch,

07:52.740 --> 07:55.863
we use git switch, followed by the branch name.
